Good plan — I’ll give you a focused checklist and suggested questions to bring to your annual with Dr. Sarah Chen so you can cover osteopenia management and whether a GI referral (Boston GI) is appropriate.

Quick context reminders (so you can mention them to Dr. Chen)
- Age: 41 (likely premenopausal unless you tell me otherwise)
- Conditions: hypertension, prior stroke
- Meds you mentioned: levothyroxine 75 mcg, intermittent prednisone taper for seasonal allergies
- Last BP: 110

Osteopenia — things to ask and discuss
- Clarify diagnosis and severity
  - “Can we review my DEXA results? What are my T-score and Z-score, and what do they mean for someone my age?”
  - If you don’t have a DEXA on file: “Should I have a DEXA scan now? If yes, which sites (hip, spine) do you recommend?”

- Menopausal status and fracture history
  - “Am I pre- or post-menopausal for the purposes of bone management? How does that change treatment?”
  - “Do I need any additional risk assessment for fracture (prior fractures, family history, low body weight)?”

- Secondary causes and medication effects
  - “Could my levothyroxine dose be affecting my bone density? Can we check TSH to ensure my replacement is appropriate?”
  - “I take prednisone occasionally for allergies — do my steroid exposures (how often/what cumulative dose) put me at risk? At what threshold would you consider treatment because of steroid use?”
  - Ask about other secondary causes to rule out: malabsorption (celiac), hyperparathyroidism, vitamin D deficiency, smoking, alcohol, rheumatoid disease.

- Lifestyle and medical management
  - “What calcium and vitamin D targets should I aim for (diet vs supplements)?”
  - “What exercise would you recommend (weight-bearing, resistance, balance)?”
  - “Should I stop or reduce any medications or supplements that affect bone health?”
  - “When is medication (bisphosphonates, denosumab, SERMs, etc.) indicated for someone my age with osteopenia?”
  - If medication is recommended: ask about side effects and any special precautions (e.g., dental exam before some agents, esophageal issues with oral bisphosphonates).

- Monitoring
  - “How often should we repeat DEXA if we start with osteopenia?”
  - “Any labs to monitor (25‑OH vitamin D, calcium, TSH, maybe PTH if abnormal calcium) and how often?”

Tests to consider requesting now
- DEXA scan (hip and lumbar spine)
- 25‑hydroxyvitamin D level
- Serum calcium and basic metabolic panel
- TSH (to ensure levothyroxine is appropriately dosed)
- Consider celiac serology or other tests if malabsorption or other risk factors present
- If long-term steroid exposure or concern: consider fracture-risk discussion and possible vertebral fracture assessment (VFA) if indicated

How prednisone use affects decisions
- Short, intermittent tapers for seasonal allergies typically confer much lower bone risk than chronic daily glucocorticoids. Tell Dr. Chen how often and what doses you take — cumulative exposure matters. Chronic use (prednisone ≳5 mg/day for ≥3 months) usually triggers proactive bone-protection measures.

When you might need a referral to Boston GI
- Colonoscopy / colorectal screening:
  - Average-risk screening generally begins at age 45 (US guideline); earlier if you have a family history of colorectal cancer (1st-degree relative <60 or multiple relatives) or red-flag symptoms (rectal bleeding, persistent change in bowel habits, iron-deficiency anemia).
  - If you want screening earlier because of family history or symptoms, ask for a referral.
- If you have chronic GERD, dysphagia, or known esophageal disease:
  - Oral bisphosphonates can worsen esophagitis; if you have reflux or swallowing problems, discuss GI evaluation before starting oral bisphosphonates.
- If you have iron-deficiency anemia, unexplained weight loss, GI bleeding, or other concerning GI symptoms — definitely request GI referral.
- If you’re on antiplatelet/anticoagulant therapy because of your stroke history, discuss implications for any endoscopic procedures (GI will coordinate peri-procedure management).

Suggested phrasing to use with Dr. Chen
- For bones: “I was told I have osteopenia. Can we review the DEXA (T-score and Z-score) and decide whether to monitor, start supplements, or consider medication? Also, could my levothyroxine or intermittent prednisone use be contributing?”
- For GI: “Do I need a GI referral now — for earlier colonoscopy or to evaluate reflux/esophageal issues before starting any bone medications like oral bisphosphonates? I’m 41 and want to be proactive given my medical history.”

What to bring or prepare for the visit
- List of current medications (dosage and frequency), including prednisone tapers (how often, doses, total days per year)
- Any prior DEXA reports or imaging
- Family history of osteoporosis or colorectal cancer
- Any GI symptoms (bleeding, anemia, reflux, dysphagia, bowel habit changes)
- Recent labs (TSH, vitamin D, calcium) if available

Next steps I can help with
- Draft a short message/email to Dr. Chen summarizing these points and requesting specific tests or a GI referral
- Create a printable checklist of questions for your appointment
- If you want, tell me how often you actually take prednisone and whether you have a family history of colon cancer or osteoporosis — I can make recommendations more tailored to your situation.

Note: This is general information to help you prepare; final decisions should be made with Dr. Chen who knows your full medical history.

