Good — I can help you prepare. Below is a focused checklist you can use at your annual with Dr. Sarah Chen plus guidance on when to get a DEXA, what labs to consider, how steroid and levothyroxine use factor in, and when a GI referral (e.g., Boston GI) is reasonable.

Quick summary of your situation (so I tailor recommendations)
- Age 41
- Hypertension (BP last 110) — on levothyroxine 75 mcg (I assume for hypothyroidism)
- Seasonal allergies treated with prednisone taper (clarify duration/frequency)
- Prior stroke (raises fall risk)
- Concerned about osteopenia management and whether you need GI referral

Important clarifying questions for me / for your visit
- Are you male or female? If female: are you menopausal or premenopausal?
- How often and for how long do you take prednisone (dose, number of courses per year)? Any chronic steroid use?
- Any prior DEXA scan results or previous low-trauma fractures?
- Current calcium / vitamin D supplements and doses?
- Any history of fractures, low BMI, smoking, or heavy alcohol use?
- Any GI symptoms (reflux/heartburn, difficulty swallowing, ulcers, GI bleeding, chronic heartburn), prior upper GI disease, or ongoing anticoagulation/NSAIDs?
- Any dental problems or planned dental procedures?

What to ask Dr. Chen (use these exact questions if helpful)
- “Given my age, stroke history, and intermittent prednisone use, should I have a DEXA scan now?”
- “Can we calculate my 10-year fracture risk (FRAX) and review whether I meet criteria for medication?”
- “Please check labs that could affect bone health: serum calcium, creatinine, 25‑hydroxyvitamin D, TSH, alkaline phosphatase — and consider PTH if calcium is abnormal.”
- “Is my levothyroxine dose appropriate (can we check TSH)? I know overreplacement can worsen bone loss.”
- “How often should we repeat DEXA if I have osteopenia (or if we start treatment)?”
- “Do my steroid exposures (current/past prednisone use) increase my need for treatment or referral?”
- “Do I need a referral to GI (Boston GI) because of GI risk factors for oral bisphosphonates or for screening because of my age/meds?”

Which tests I’d recommend discussing / ordering at the visit
- DEXA scan (spine + hip) — consider now if you have any of: prior low-trauma fracture, chronic glucocorticoid use (see below), signs of secondary osteoporosis, or major risk factors (low BMI, heavy smoking, high alcohol, strong family history).
- Labs: TSH (ensure not overtreated on levothyroxine), serum calcium, creatinine (renal function), 25‑OH vitamin D, alkaline phosphatase. Consider PTH if calcium abnormal. Consider CBC, CMP if looking for secondary causes.
- FRAX fracture risk calculation (with/without BMD depending on DEXA availability).

When DEXA is recommended at your age
- Typical screening starts later for many people, but do DEXA now if you have:
  - chronic glucocorticoid therapy (commonly defined as ≥5 mg prednisone/day for ≥3 months),
  - previous low-trauma fracture,
  - clinical risk factors for secondary osteoporosis (e.g., hyperthyroidism or overreplacement with levothyroxine, malabsorption, hypogonadism),
  - strong family history or other risk factors that increase fracture risk,
  - significant fall risk after stroke.

How prednisone and levothyroxine affect bones
- Repeated or chronic systemic steroids increase bone loss and fracture risk; short “bursts” (a single short taper) have less effect, but cumulative exposure matters. Tell Dr. Chen how many courses/year and doses.
- Excess levothyroxine (TSH suppressed) can accelerate bone loss. Ask for TSH/target range.

When to consider medication (bisphosphonates, denosumab, etc.)
- Typical treatment indications: T-score ≤ -2.5 (osteoporosis), or T-score between -1.0 and -2.5 (osteopenia) with high FRAX risk (10-year hip fracture ≥3% or major osteoporotic fracture ≥20%), or chronic glucocorticoid use at fracture risk.
- If you have GI disease that makes oral bisphosphonates unsuitable, options include IV zoledronic acid or subcutaneous denosumab — discuss those alternatives with your PCP or specialist.

When to refer to GI (Boston GI) — reasonable scenarios
- Ongoing significant GERD, dysphagia, or known esophageal disease (Barrett’s, strictures) — relevant because oral bisphosphonates can irritate the esophagus and you might need alternative osteoporosis treatments or GI evaluation.
- History of significant peptic ulcer disease, recurrent GI bleeding, or chronic NSAID use with symptoms.
- Malabsorption syndromes (chronic diarrhea, celiac disease suspicion) that could affect bone density or vitamin absorption.
- If you have unexplained iron-deficiency anemia or concerning GI symptoms that need specialist workup.
- If you want a lower-threshold colonoscopy or GI evaluation for other reasons (note: routine colon cancer screening generally starts at age 45, unless other risk factors exist).

Practical phrasing for requesting a DEXA and labs from your PCP
- “Please order a DEXA (lumbar spine and hip) and FRAX calculation and these labs: TSH, calcium, creatinine, 25‑OH vitamin D, alkaline phosphatase. Also check whether my prednisone exposure means we should treat rather than just monitor.”
- “If I’m a poor candidate for oral bisphosphonates (esophageal or GERD issues), can you refer me to GI to decide if oral therapy is safe or if IV/other therapy is better?”

Lifestyle and monitoring recommendations you can mention
- Aim for calcium intake ~1000–1200 mg/day from diet/supplements as needed; vitamin D supplementation (often 800–2000 IU/day depending on levels and provider guidance).
- Weight‑bearing and resistance exercise, smoking cessation, limit alcohol, fall-prevention strategies (home safety; physical therapy if post-stroke balance/gait deficits).
- Repeat DEXA interval: often 1–2 years if starting treatment or high risk; 2–3 years if lower risk — confirm with Dr. Chen.

Red flags to bring up at the visit
- Recent low-trauma fracture
- Multiple prednisone courses or long courses
- Symptoms of reflux, difficulty swallowing, or GI bleeding
- Suppressed TSH or symptoms of hyperthyroid

Would you like any of the following next?
- I can draft a concise message/email to Dr. Chen requesting these tests and a DEXA.
- I can produce a one-page script/printout you can bring to the visit.
- I can help draft exact wording to request a referral to Boston GI if you confirm GI symptoms or reasons.

Tell me: your sex/menopausal status, how often and what dose/duration of prednisone you take, and whether you’ve had a prior DEXA or fractures — and I’ll tailor the script and recommendations (including whether a DEXA or GI referral is more strongly indicated).

