  - Thyroid hormone (levothyroxine) can accelerate bone loss if over-replacement (suppressive dosing). 75 mcg is a common replacement dose but check recent TSH to ensure you are not overtreated.
  - Other risk factors: female sex, low BMI, smoking, heavy alcohol use, family history of hip/vertebral fracture, prior fragility fracture, vitamin D deficiency, and some chronic diseases (celiac, rheumatoid arthritis, hypogonadism).
- Typical screening recommendations:
  - Routine DEXA screening is usually recommended for women ≥65 and men ≥70, or younger people with clinical risk factors.
  - For adults under 50–60, DEXA is recommended earlier when major risk factors are present — especially chronic glucocorticoid use, a prior fragility fracture, or other secondary causes of bone loss.
- What to consider now for you:
  - If your prednisone exposure is just a short seasonal taper each year (brief courses), your risk is lower; if you take repeated courses or chronic low-dose prednisone, you should be evaluated (DEXA + labs) and may need treatment.
  - Check TSH to ensure levothyroxine dose is not suppressive.
  - Check 25-hydroxyvitamin D and serum calcium, and consider basic labs to rule out secondary causes (CBC, CMP, creatinine, thyroid tests, possibly celiac screening depending on history).
  - Consider a DEXA scan now if you have repeated steroid courses, prior fracture, family history of early osteoporosis, or other risk factors — otherwise discuss timing with PCP.

When to consider pharmacologic treatment
- If DEXA shows osteoporosis (T-score ≤ -2.5) ⇒ treat.
- If DEXA shows osteopenia (T-score between -1.0 and -2.5), decision uses FRAX (10-year fracture risk): in many guidelines, treatment is offered when 10-year major osteoporotic fracture risk ≥20% or hip fracture risk ≥3% (thresholds may vary by guideline). Chronic glucocorticoid use increases the treatment threshold.
- For glucocorticoid-induced osteoporosis, guidelines often recommend initiating bone-protective therapy (usually a bisphosphonate) for adults expected to be on ≥5 mg prednisone daily for ≥3 months and judged at increased fracture risk.
- If a medication is recommended, first-line agents are oral bisphosphonates (alendronate, risedronate). Discuss renal function, esophageal disease, dental procedures, and long-term monitoring.

Lifestyle and preventive measures you can start now
- Weight-bearing/resistance exercise, fall prevention, balance training.
- Ensure adequate calcium intake (diet first; typical total 1000–1200 mg/day depending on age and sex) and vitamin D (many clinicians use 800–2000 IU/day pending level; replete if deficient).
- Stop smoking and limit alcohol (≥3 drinks/day increases risk).
- Review thyroid dosing; avoid chronic over-replacement.
- Minimize cumulative glucocorticoid exposure if possible; discuss alternatives for allergy control with your PCP/allergist.

Questions and requests to bring to Dr. Sarah Chen (short list you can read aloud)
- “Given my history (41, stroke history, levothyroxine 75 mcg, and intermittent prednisone), do you recommend a DEXA scan now or wait? If so, can you order one?”
- “Can we check TSH, 25-hydroxyvitamin D, basic metabolic panel and calcium, and CBC to evaluate bone-health risks and secondary causes?”
- “How much prednisone have I actually had cumulatively this year/in recent years? Does my steroid exposure meet the threshold to start bone-protective treatment?”
- “If my DEXA shows osteopenia, how do you calculate my fracture risk (FRAX) and what fracture-risk threshold would you use to start medication?”
- “If treatment indicated, what are first-line options here (bisphosphonates vs alternatives), and what are the benefits/risks for my situation?”
- “Can you review my levothyroxine dosing and recent TSH? Is my dose appropriate for bone health?”
- “Would you like me to see Endocrinology for bone-health management or medication decisions, or can you start/monitor treatment here?”

What to bring or have ready for the visit
- A current medication list with exact prednisone doses and dates (how many courses, how long each).
- Any prior DEXA reports (if applicable) or fracture history.
- Family history of osteoporosis or hip fracture.
- Recent labs (TSH, vitamin D) if available.
- Info on tobacco/alcohol, exercise, height/weight or BMI.

Specific guidance about a Boston GI referral
- If by “Boston GI” you mean colonoscopy screening:
  - Current U.S. recommendations generally start average-risk colorectal cancer screening at age 45. At age 41, routine colonoscopy generally isn’t required unless you have a family history of early-onset colorectal cancer (first-degree relative <60), personal GI symptoms (rectal bleeding, persistent unexplained iron-deficiency anemia, chronic unexplained abdominal pain/weight loss), or other high-risk conditions (IBD, hereditary syndromes).
  - Ask: “Do I need colonoscopy now based on family history or my medical history, or can we wait until 45?”
- If you mean GI referral for symptoms or bleeding risk:
  - Request referral if you have GI symptoms (reflux uncontrolled by OTC meds, dysphagia, GI bleeding, iron-deficiency anemia, persistent abdominal pain) or if you’re on anticoagulation with concerning bleeding.
  - Chronic or repeated high-dose steroids do not by themselves require routine GI referral unless you have GI symptoms or are taking NSAIDs concurrently — but a history of PUD, GI bleeding, or H. pylori would warrant GI evaluation.
- If you want a proactive referral: say “I’d like a GI referral for evaluation and to discuss whether I should have colonoscopy screening earlier because of my medical history” and list specific reasons (family history, anemia, symptoms).

Suggested phrasing to ask Dr. Chen (one-liners)
- For DEXA & bone evaluation: “Could you order a DEXA and labs (TSH, 25-OH vitamin D, CMP, CBC) so we can assess my bone health today? I’m concerned because of prednisone use and thyroid replacement.”
- For GI/colonoscopy referral: “Do I need a GI referral for colonoscopy now, or can screening wait until 45? Please refer me to Boston GI if you think earlier evaluation is warranted.”

Red flags — contact PCP/ED promptly if you have
- New severe back pain with height loss (possible vertebral fracture), new unexplained bruising or bleeding, black/tarry stools, or new focal neurologic symptoms given stroke history.

If you’d like, I can:
- Draft a succinct message or patient portal note to Dr. Chen requesting DEXA and the labs, and asking whether a Boston GI referral is appropriate — include your medication list and prednisone exposure.
- Or we can prepare a printable one-page checklist of the questions above for your appointment.

Which would you prefer, and did you want me to draft the message to Dr. Chen now? Also please tell me whether your prednisone use is intermittent/brief or repeated/chronic and whether you have any GI symptoms or a family history of colon cancer.

