- Any prior DEXA (bone density) scan and when/what the T-scores were?
- How often do you take prednisone (single short tapers once/season, or repeated courses, or ongoing low-dose)? Any other chronic steroid use?
- Are you on antiplatelet (aspirin) or anticoagulant after your stroke?
- Any GI symptoms (heartburn, dysphagia, reflux, chronic NSAID use, black/tarry stools, iron-deficiency anemia)?
- Any family history of colorectal cancer or polyps (first-degree relative <60 or multiple relatives)?

B — High-value questions to ask Dr. Sarah Chen at the annual
- “Given my history (stroke, hypertension, levothyroxine 75 mcg, intermittent prednisone for allergies), should I get a DEXA scan now?”
- “Can we calculate my 10‑year fracture risk (FRAX) now? If it’s high, what treatment would you recommend?”
- “How much prednisone have I taken cumulatively this year? Does that change my bone risk?”
- “Please check my TSH (to make sure I’m not overtreated on levothyroxine), 25‑OH vitamin D, calcium, and basic metabolic panel (creatinine) if we are considering osteoporosis therapy.”
- “Do I need any GI workup (referral to Boston GI) for colon cancer screening, reflux, or before starting bisphosphonates?”
- “If my DEXA shows osteopenia, what lifestyle changes or medications would you recommend? Any contraindications for oral bisphosphonate in my case?”

C — Tests you should consider ordering at/around the visit
- DEXA scan of lumbar spine and hip (baseline if you have risk factors or steroid exposure)
- FRAX score (can be calculated in clinic once DEXA + risk factors known)
- Labs: 25‑hydroxyvitamin D, serum calcium, serum creatinine (for kidney function), TSH (because you’re on levothyroxine), and if indicated, CBC/iron studies (if GI blood loss suspected)
- If DEXA abnormal or secondary cause suspected: consider serum protein electrophoresis, celiac testing, or other targeted labs per Dr. Chen

D — Osteopenia management: practical steps and when to start medication
- When to treat with medication:
  - DEXA T-score ≤ −2.5 = osteoporosis → pharmacologic therapy recommended.
  - Osteopenia (T-score −1.0 to −2.5): consider treatment if 10-year FRAX major osteoporotic fracture risk ≥20% or hip fracture risk ≥3%, or if you have a prior fragility fracture, or chronic glucocorticoid exposure (prednisone ≥5 mg/day for ≥3 months).
  - Short intermittent prednisone tapers (brief seasonal courses) usually carry much lower risk than chronic daily steroids — note cumulative exposure.
- First-line medications:
  - Oral bisphosphonates (alendronate, risedronate) are common first-line agents unless contraindicated (esophageal disease, inability to sit upright after dosing, or very low kidney function).
  - IV options (zoledronic acid) if oral not tolerated or adherence concerns.
  - Alternatives include denosumab for some patients; choice depends on renal function, risk profile, and preferences.
- Baseline and monitoring:
  - Ensure adequate vitamin D (aim for at least 20–30 ng/mL; high-risk patients often targeted ≥30 ng/mL) and dietary calcium (about 1,000 mg/day for most adults; 1,200 mg for postmenopausal women).
  - Encourage weight-bearing exercise, smoking cessation, limit alcohol (ideally <2 drinks/day, lower if fracture risk), fall-prevention measures.
  - If starting bisphosphonate: check creatinine/eGFR and review esophageal symptoms (if present, you may need GI evaluation or IV therapy).
  - Repeat DEXA usually every 1–2 years when initiating or changing therapy; less frequently if stable.

E — Do you need a GI referral to Boston GI now?
- Likely not solely because you’re 41. Routine colorectal cancer screening usually starts at age 45 (USPSTF) for average risk; you’d refer earlier if you have high-risk family history or red-flag symptoms.
- Scenarios where GI referral now makes sense:
  - You have GI symptoms: new or worsening reflux, dysphagia, persistent heartburn, GI bleeding, iron‑deficiency anemia, melena, or hematochezia.
  - Prior positive fecal occult blood test or abnormal labs suggesting GI blood loss.
  - Family history of colorectal cancer in a first-degree relative <60 or multiple affected relatives — then earlier colonoscopy may be recommended.
  - If you have significant esophageal disease (severe reflux, Barrett’s esophagus, or dysphagia) and you are considering oral bisphosphonates — an evaluation makes sense because oral bisphosphonates can irritate the esophagus and are contraindicated in some esophageal conditions.
  - Long-term PPI or NSAID use causing ulcers or symptoms.
- If none of the above apply (no GI symptoms, no high-risk family history, only intermittent short prednisone tapers), a GI referral is probably unnecessary now. You can discuss the specifics with Dr. Chen and refer only if symptoms or risk factors are present.

Next steps I can help with
