Good plan — I’ll break this into (A) what matters for osteopenia at your age and with your meds/history, (B) when a GI referral (e.g., to Boston GI) is reasonable, and (C) concrete things to ask Dr. Chen and tests you can request at your annual visit. If you want, I can also draft a short message you could send to her before the visit.

A. Osteopenia / bone-health — key points for someone age 41 with your history
- Important risk modifiers you already have:
  - Prednisone use: even intermittent or repeated short courses can matter if you take frequent bursts; chronic glucocorticoid therapy (commonly defined ≥5 mg prednisone daily for ≥3 months) is a clear indication for earlier bone evaluation and possibly treatment.
  - Levothyroxine: overtreatment (excess thyroid hormone / low TSH) can accelerate bone loss — check TSH and dose.
  - Prior stroke: increases fall risk (and therefore fracture risk) if you have balance, mobility, or weakness issues.
- Typical screening timing:
  - Routine DEXA screening is usually recommended at older ages (women ≥65, men ≥70) unless there are risk factors. At 41, you would usually only get a DEXA earlier if you have secondary causes of bone loss (chronic glucocorticoid exposure, hypogonadism, prior fragility fracture, known secondary causes).
  - That said, with prednisone exposure or other secondary risks, an earlier DEXA is reasonable.
- What to check now (labs/tests)
  - DEXA scan (hip + spine) — if prednisone use is frequent/chronic or other risk factors
  - Blood tests: CBC, basic metabolic panel (calcium, creatinine), 25‑OH vitamin D, TSH (to check levothyroxine dosing), and iron studies if concerned about GI blood loss/anemia
  - Consider PTH and phosphorus if calcium abnormal; bone turnover markers are optional.
  - Calculate/consider FRAX score (often needs DEXA) to quantify 10-year fracture risk.
- Lifestyle/first-line measures (what you can start regardless)
  - Calcium: aim for ~1000–1200 mg/day from diet ± supplements as needed.
  - Vitamin D: typical targets 800–2000 IU/day; check 25‑OH D level and replete if low (target generally >30 ng/mL, per many labs/guidelines).
  - Weight-bearing and resistance exercise; balance training if stroke affected mobility.
  - Avoid smoking and limit alcohol.
  - Fall-risk assessment (home safety, assistive device, PT if gait/balance problems).
- When to consider medications (e.g., bisphosphonates)
  - Pharmacologic treatment is usually reserved for high fracture risk (FRAX threshold), documented osteoporosis on DEXA (T-score ≤ −2.5), prior fragility fracture, or chronic glucocorticoid therapy at risk thresholds.
  - If you are on chronic steroids (see definition above), many guidelines recommend starting bone-protective therapy (often bisphosphonate) or at least close monitoring.
  - Discuss risks/benefits (renal function, dental health, rare side effects) with Dr. Chen/endocrinology if treatment is being considered.

B. When to refer to GI (Boston GI) given your age and risk factors
- Routine colorectal cancer screening usually begins at age 45 for average risk (US recommendations), so at age 41 a routine referral for screening colonoscopy is usually not required unless:
  - Family history of colorectal cancer in a first-degree relative diagnosed <60, or multiple affected relatives (in which case earlier screening/referral is indicated).
  - You have GI symptoms: rectal bleeding, black/tarry stools, unexplained change in bowel habits, unexplained weight loss, persistent abdominal pain, or iron-deficiency anemia.
  - Positive fecal immunochemical test (FIT) or stool blood test.
  - Personal history of inflammatory bowel disease.
- Other reasons to refer to GI earlier:
  - Unexplained iron-deficiency anemia (look for occult GI bleeding).
  - Chronic GERD with alarm symptoms (dysphagia, weight loss, bleeding) — may need endoscopy.
  - If you are (or will be) on chronic NSAIDs or on anticoagulants/antiplatelets and you have GI symptoms or history of peptic disease, you may need assessment.
- Specific to your meds/history:
  - Prednisone and short steroid bursts alone don’t necessitate GI referral unless you have GI symptoms or bleeding risk.
  - If you’re taking antiplatelet agents or anticoagulants (you didn’t list these) after stroke, that raises the importance of evaluating any GI bleeding or anemia.

C. Concrete questions and requests to bring to Dr. Sarah Chen (scripts and checklist)
1) Questions to ask
- “Given my prednisone use (describe how often/duration) and my levothyroxine, do you recommend a DEXA scan now or later?”
- “Should we check labs today: 25‑OH vitamin D, calcium, TSH, CBC, and iron studies?”
- “Is my levothyroxine dose appropriate for bone health (can we review my most recent TSH)?”
- “Do I meet criteria for bone-protective medication now, or would you prefer monitoring and lifestyle measures first?”
- “Do you think I need a fall-risk assessment or PT because of my prior stroke?”
- “Do I need a GI referral now (e.g., colonoscopy or other workup)? Are there any GI symptoms or family-history details you need from me to decide?”
2) Tests / orders you can ask the PCP to place if appropriate
- DEXA (hip + lumbar spine)
- Labs: 25‑OH vitamin D, TSH, CBC, basic metabolic panel (BMP), iron studies (ferritin, iron/TIBC) if anemia suspected
- FRAX calculation (often done after DEXA)
- If GI concerns: fecal occult/fecal immunochemical test (FIT) or referral for colonoscopy if indicated
3) Information to have ready for the visit (helps decision-making)
- Exact prednisone history: how many courses per year? typical dose and duration (e.g., 40 mg × 5 days, 10–14 days, every season?)
- Any history of fractures from low-impact falls
- Family history of osteoporosis or early colorectal cancer (relative and age at diagnosis)
- Any GI symptoms (blood in stool, dark stools, change in bowel habits, abdominal pain, heartburn, difficulty swallowing)
- Current medications including any aspirin / anticoagulants / NSAIDs
4) Sample phrasing for the appointment or a message to Dr. Chen
- Short script: “Hi Dr. Chen — ahead of my annual, I wanted to review bone health and whether I should have a DEXA. I take levothyroxine 75 mcg and have intermittent prednisone tapers for seasonal allergies; I also have a history of stroke. Can we check TSH, 25‑OH vitamin D, CBC and consider a DEXA if you think it’s indicated? Also, should I be referred to GI now for colon evaluation, or only if I have symptoms/family history? Thanks.”
- Tell me if you want me to draft this as an email/message to send.

D. Red flags that would make me recommend an urgent GI referral
- Any evidence of GI bleeding (black/tarry stools, bright red blood), iron-deficiency anemia on labs, new or progressive dysphagia, unintentional weight loss, or persistent severe abdominal pain.

E. Quick actionable summary
- If your prednisone use is only rare short single short tapers (e.g., one short course/year), immediate DEXA or meds are less likely needed — still check TSH and vitamin D and review fall risk.
- If prednisone is frequent or chronic (≥5 mg daily for months or many recurrent high-dose bursts), ask for a DEXA now and discuss bone-protective treatment.
- You usually don’t need a GI referral just because you’re 41 unless you have family history, symptoms, or anemia; otherwise routine colon cancer screening starts at ~45.

If you want, I can:
- Draft a short message to Dr. Chen with the questions and test requests above.
- Help you document your prednisone history and current meds into a one-page summary to bring to the visit.
Which would you prefer?

